### What is this question really testing?

Decision-making under uncertainty. The interviewer is checking that you do not freeze waiting for perfect information, and that you do not gamble carelessly.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class UncertaintyTest {
    enum Behavior { CALIBRATED, FROZEN, RECKLESS }
    public static Behavior evaluate(boolean gathered, boolean assessedRisk, boolean decided) {
        if (!decided) return Behavior.FROZEN;
        if (!assessedRisk) return Behavior.RECKLESS;
        return Behavior.CALIBRATED;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true)); // CALIBRATED
    }
}
```

### What was the situation?

Monitoring showed a spike in failed transfers at National Bank of India, with early signs some might be double-processing. I did not know how many accounts were affected or the root cause.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class UncertainSituation {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(UncertainSituation.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.warning("Failed transfers spiking");
        log.warning("Possible double-processing on some accounts");
        log.info("Unknown: how many accounts? What is the root cause?");
        log.info("Decision needed: keep running, throttle, or pause?");
    }
}
```

### How did you assess each option?

For each option, reason about the worst case if you are wrong and whether the choice is reversible.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class OptionAssessment {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Keep running: worst case = widespread double-charge (irreversible)");
        System.out.println("Throttle: worst case = slow transfers (partially reversible)");
        System.out.println("Pause: worst case = brief availability hit (fully reversible)");
        System.out.println("Under uncertainty with money: safest reversible option wins.");
    }
}
```

### Why did you choose to pause?

A short pause is easy to undo. An uncontrolled double-charge is not. Under uncertainty with money on the line, the safest reversible option wins.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class PauseDecision {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(PauseDecision.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Decision: PAUSE transfers");
        log.info("Reasoning: safest reversible option; brief availability hit is cheap to undo");
        log.info("Assumption: issue may be widespread; trading availability for safety");
        log.info("Checkpoint: revisit in 15 minutes when we know the blast radius");
    }
}
```

### How did you communicate your assumptions?

Explicitly stated what you were assuming so the team could challenge it.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class ExplicitAssumptions {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Assumption 1: double-processing may be widespread");
        System.out.println("Assumption 2: pausing is safer than letting possibly-bad transfers through");
        System.out.println("Assumption 3: root cause will be identified within 30 minutes");
        System.out.println("Team can challenge any of these.");
    }
}
```

### How did you adjust as new information arrived?

Within minutes confirmed the issue was limited, fixed the root cause, and resumed transfers.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class AdjustWithData {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(AdjustWithData.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("15 min later: confirmed issue limited to 12 accounts");
        log.info("Root cause: race condition in new retry logic");
        log.info("Fix deployed, transfers resumed");
        log.info("No widespread double-charge thanks to early pause");
    }
}
```

### What is the one-way vs two-way door framework?

A one-way-door decision (database engine, public API) deserves deep deliberation. A two-way-door decision (feature flag, internal config) deserves speed.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class DoorFramework {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("One-way door (irreversible): invest more in reducing uncertainty");
        System.out.println("Two-way door (reversible): move fast, adjust later");
        System.out.println("Pausing transfers is two-way: easy to resume = fast decision");
    }
}
```

### What was the outcome?

Prevented any large-scale double-charge. Brief pause, quick fix, transfers resumed.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class UncertaintyOutcome {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Double-charges prevented: 0 widespread impact");
        System.out.println("Downtime: ~15 minutes (brief, fully reversible)");
        System.out.println("Root cause fixed permanently");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class UncertaintyFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("What if you were wrong to pause? -> Brief availability hit, easily resumed");
        System.out.println("What if you had not paused? -> Possible widespread double-charge");
        System.out.println("Would you do the same? -> Yes, safest reversible option under uncertainty");
    }
}
```
